Craps ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every round of craps follows a two-step sequence governed by a marker puck (which reads OFF or ON):

1. The Come-Out Roll

• The round begins with a come-out roll.
• Pass Line wins: If the shooter rolls a 7 or 11.
• Pass Line loses (Craps): If the shooter rolls a 2, 3, or 12. (Note: On the Don’t Pass line, 2 or 3 wins and 12 is a tie/push).
• Establish the Point: If the shooter rolls a 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, or 10, that number becomes the "Point". The dealer flips the puck to ON and places it on that number.

2. The Point Phase

• Once the Point is set, the shooter continues rolling the dice.
• To Win (Make the Point): If the shooter re-rolls the Point number before rolling a 7, Pass Line bets win. The puck flips back to OFF, and a new come-out roll begins with the same shooter.
• To Lose (Seven Out): If the shooter rolls a 7 before re-rolling the Point, Pass Line bets lose. This is called "sevening out," and the dice pass clockwise to the next player.

